<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Breathing to Relax</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer opinions in a simple presentation or lecture. Interactive: Participate in group discussions on various topics, including local and global interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can infer and discuss opinions expressed in simple lectures on environmental topics (e.g., "Visiting the Sendero Los Quetzales in Volcán Barú National Park helps people feel calmer and more relaxed due to the serene natural environment."). Can participate in group discussions on global topics by sharing opinions (e.g., "I think stress affects people's mental health by making them feel overwhelmed. Practicing mindfulness could really help reduce stress and improve focus."). Can actively participate in group discussions on local and global topics related to managing stress and mindfulness by asking clarifying questions to understand better others' opinions (e.g., "Why are more students practicing mindfulness these days?" "People are practicing mindfulness to stay calm during stressful times.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to close their eyes for 30 seconds to focus on their breathing. After, the teacher shows pictures of serene natural environments (e.g., Sendero Los Quetzales, Parque Omar) and asks: "What feelings do these pictures evoke?" and "How can connecting with nature help manage stress?" Introduce key vocabulary words like <i>relaxation, mindfulness, calm, serene, deep breathing, peaceful, nature, quiet spot</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of target vocabulary, especially the long vowel sound /uː/ in words like "soothing" and "smooth," and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words such as /ˈmaɪnd.fəl.nəs/ (mindfulness) and /ˌben.ɪˈfɪʃ.əl/ (beneficial). The teacher also models phrases for giving advice using modals: "You should try deep breathing." and for sharing techniques: "Practicing mindfulness helps me relax."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'mindfulness' about thinking of many things or focusing on the present moment?" (Expected answer: "Focusing on the present moment.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'You should practice yoga,' am I giving an order or advice?" (Expected answer: "Advice.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will listen to a short (2-3 minute) audio segment from a podcast titled "Panama Wellness: Embracing Nature for Relief," where two young Panamanians discuss their experiences with stress and various relaxation techniques. The segment features opinions on forest bathing and guided meditation, mentioning how "Practicing mindfulness has helped many people improve their focus and reduce stress." and "Visiting the Sendero Los Quetzales in Volcán Barú National Park helps people feel calmer and more relaxed." (Authentic audio recording - B1.2 level, informal interview register).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer opinions in a simple presentation or lecture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> First listening. "What is the main topic of the podcast segment?" (Expected answer: "Different ways young people in Panama manage stress and relax.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Second listening. Students listen and mark statements as True or False based on the speakers' opinions. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"One speaker believes forest bathing is just a trend and not truly beneficial." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Both speakers agree that regular exercise promotes a stress-free mind." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"The podcast suggests that music helps people to focus better." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Inferring Opinions:</b> Third listening. Students identify specific phrases that convey opinions. For example, "The speaker thinks that *spending time in nature* is an *effective technique* for *stress relief*."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details, identifying gerunds used as nouns, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends. Vocabulary: <i>breathing, relaxation, forest bathing, biohack, mindfulness, meditation, stress, wellness, nature, focus, music, exercise, techniques, practices, relieve, manage, benefit, promote, try, suggest, calm, reduce, improve, soothing, beneficial, calming, helpful, stress-free, peaceful, serene, stressful, overwhelmed</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> The teacher distributes a partial transcript of the audio segment with gaps for target vocabulary (e.g., <i>mindfulness, deep breathing, soothing</i>) and grammatical structures (gerunds, modals). Students listen to the audio a fourth time and complete the gaps. Example: "Many people find __________ beneficial for managing daily __________." or "You __________ try __________ in a __________ spot."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher plays short excerpts from the audio focusing on advice or trends. Students listen and complete sentences using the target grammar and vocabulary. Example prompt: "The speaker mentioned that people are currently __________ to stay calm." (Students complete: "...are currently *practicing mindfulness* to stay calm.") Another example: "To reduce stress, you __________." (Students complete: "...you *should try listening to soothing music*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Opinion Sharing):</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers from the previous activities. They discuss *why* certain words fit and *what* opinions were expressed, using phrases like: "I think the speaker's opinion is that..." and "Do you agree that *practicing meditation* helps with *focus*?" They are encouraged to ask clarifying questions like, "Why are more students practicing mindfulness these days?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Discussion & Advice Giving.</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students will listen to two distinct short audio clips (1 minute each) describing different stress-inducing scenarios relevant to 11th graders (e.g., academic pressure, social media overwhelm). After each clip, groups will discuss the scenario, infer the feelings/opinions expressed by the person in the clip, and collectively offer advice on how to manage the stress using at least two different relaxation techniques learned (<i>mindfulness, deep breathing, listening to soothing music, forest bathing, exercise, meditation</i>). They must use gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends in their discussion. For example, "I think stress affects people's mental health by making them feel overwhelmed. Practicing mindfulness could really help reduce stress and improve focus." or "You could try relaxation techniques like deep breathing." They should also share personal wellness strategies: "In my experience, exercise helps with focus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, specifically inferring opinions from the scenarios and effectively giving advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher will circulate during the group discussions in Stage 4, using a checklist to observe students' ability to: 1) Infer opinions from the audio scenarios, 2) Participate in group discussions, 3) Use target vocabulary (e.g., <i>mindfulness, beneficial, soothe</i>), 4) Use target grammar (gerunds, modals for advice, present continuous for trends), and 5) Offer polite suggestions and share personal experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Group discussions and participation in sharing advice. (No written product for this listening-focused lesson, but active verbal participation is key).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /uː/ and multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective inference of opinions from listening input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary related to stress management and relaxation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative participation in group discussions, including asking clarifying questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence when giving advice and sharing personal experiences.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List one new relaxation technique or piece of advice you learned today from a classmate or the audio that you found helpful."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel identifying opinions in an English audio presentation now (1-5, with 5 being very confident)?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to the opinions in English today, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which stress management technique discussed today do you think is most beneficial for students in Panama, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The warm-up successfully activated prior knowledge and introduced key vocabulary. The audio material was appropriate for B1.2, but some students struggled with inferring nuanced opinions on the first listen; multiple listens were crucial. The group discussions effectively promoted interaction, and students generally used gerunds and modals well when giving advice. Some students needed more encouragement to ask clarifying questions. Adjustments for next time: Provide a more explicit list of opinion-inferring phrases before the presentation stage. Scaffold the advice-giving with more sentence frames for polite suggestions. Perhaps pre-teach more complex vocabulary like "biohack" more thoroughly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts related to stress management and relaxation through listening. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read an article or blog post that reinforces these same vocabulary words and further explores the scenarios of "Managing Stress in Changing Times," allowing them to see the words in written context and deepen their understanding before moving to active speaking and writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>